@prefix ds: <https://ds.canonical.com/>.
@prefix anatomy: <https://anatomy.canonical.com/>.
@prefix dt: <https://dt.canonical.com/>.

ds:apps.subcomponent.data_table-group_header_row a ds:Subcomponent;
    ds:name "DataTable.GroupHeaderRow";
    ds:summary "A group header row is a collapsible row that separates groups of data rows based on a shared column value. It spans the full width of the table and is labeled with the column name and its value (for example, \"Status: In progress\"). It contains a chevron indicating open/closed state, the group label text, and an optional selection checkbox when selection is enabled.";
    ds:anatomyDsl "";
    ds:anatomyClassic "https://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=3340-2397&t=8RN2Hpk8UX3gMX3u-4\n\nhttps://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=4817-88534&t=2fO23I8WsyT0YVJ1-4";
    ds:usage "Grouping organizes table rows into collapsible sections based on a shared column value. It makes categorical structure visible directly in the table, so users can scan, focus on, and act on subsets of data without relying on filters or manual scanning. Consider grouping when users already think about the data in categories. For example, a table of support cases is naturally understood by severity, and a table of deployments is naturally understood by environment. Grouping reflects that mental model in the table layout itself.\n\nGrouping works best with medium-to-large datasets where a flat list becomes hard to navigate. It is less useful when the dataset is small or when the user’s primary task is comparing individual rows across categories, since grouping separates rows into sections that make cross-group comparison harder.\n\n## When to use\n\n- Users frequently scan or act on rows by a categorical attribute such as status, priority, or region.\n- The dataset is large enough that a flat list makes it hard to find relevant rows.\n- The data has a natural hierarchy that users already think in.\n\n## When not to use\n\n- The dataset is small enough that users can scan all rows without difficulty.\n- The primary task is comparing individual rows across categories. Sorting is a better fit.\n- The table uses keyset pagination. Grouping is incompatible with keyset pagination. Refer to the pagination with groups section for details.\n- No column has a small, known set of values. Grouping only works with enumerated values.\n\n## What can be grouped\n\nGroups are always based on columns in the table. Rows are grouped by a value in a column.\n\n- Groups can **only** be created for columns with **enumerated values**, where the value can only be one out of a set of known options (for example, “In progress”, “Finished”, “Queued”).\n- Groups **cannot** be created for columns with unique data, because that would produce many groups containing only one row. For such columns, sorting is more appropriate.\n- Tables **can** be grouped by a column that is hidden. The column does not need to be visible for grouping to work.\n\nWhen designing a table, the designer chooses which columns support grouping.\n\n## Order of groups\n\nThe order of groups is **alphabetical by default**. The designer can choose a custom order if there is meaning associated with a specific sequence. For example, a Status column could be ordered from start to finish: “In progress”, “Success”, “Failed”.\n\n## Empty groups\n\nGroups with no items are not shown. If a group has no content, the group header row is hidden entirely.\n\n## One active grouping at a time\n\nA table can only be grouped by one column at a time. If the user activates grouping on a different column, the previous grouping is deactivated and replaced by the new one.";
    ds:guidelines "# Interactions\n\n## Choosing which column to group by\n\nhttps://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=3486-39979&t=2PBanDq6QA2yNcI2-4\n\nThe user selects the grouping column through the **table settings panel**.\n\n- Not all columns support grouping. Columns that do display a **“Group by” button** next to them in the settings panel.\n- Selecting the “Group by” button activates grouping for that column. The button enters an **active state**.\n- Since the table needs to be restructured and new data likely fetched, the table enters a **full skeleton loading state** while grouping is applied.\n- After loading completes, the table displays the grouped data.\n- Selecting an active “Group by” button again **deactivates** grouping. The table enters a loading state and then returns to an ungrouped view.\n- If the user selects a different column’s “Group by” button while one is already active, the previous grouping is deactivated and the new one is activated.\n- A table may offer multiple columns that support grouping.\n\n## Expand and collapse\n\nhttps://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=3486-50231&t=2PBanDq6QA2yNcI2-4\n\nhttps://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=3486-51205&t=2PBanDq6QA2yNcI2-4\n\nSelecting the group header row toggles the group between expanded and collapsed states. The chevron direction updates accordingly (down for open, right for closed).\n\nCollapsing a group hides its child rows, which means new rows may need to be loaded into the freed space. The loading behavior depends on how the group relates to pagination:\n\n- **Group spans multiple pages:** When a multi-page group is collapsed, skeleton loading placeholders appear beneath the collapsed group header while new data is fetched to fill the page.\n- **Group fits on the current page:** When a single-page group is collapsed, the loading state appears only toward the bottom of the page where new items load in. The other groups and their rows remain visible above.\n\nAfter loading completes, the collapsed group shows only its header row. The remaining space is filled with rows from subsequent groups.\n\n## Sorting within groups\n\nhttps://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=3486-49256&t=2PBanDq6QA2yNcI2-4\n\nWhen grouping is enabled, sorting applies **only within each group**. There is no sorting across groups.\n\n- **Without pagination (single-page groups):** Sorting is straightforward with no loading state needed. Rows within each group reorder immediately.\n- **With multi-page groups:** A loading state is required while new sorted rows are fetched. The group header remains visible with skeleton loading placeholders replacing the child rows until the sorted data arrives.\n\n## Pagination with groups\n\n\n\nhttps://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=3486-42366&t=2PBanDq6QA2yNcI2-4\n\nhttps://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=3486-42596&t=2PBanDq6QA2yNcI2-4\n\nhttps://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=3486-42826&t=2PBanDq6QA2yNcI2-4\n\nIf a group spans multiple pages, the **group header row is repeated** at the top of each subsequent page that contains rows from that group. For example, if the “In progress” group starts on page 1 and its last 3 items appear on page 2, the “In progress” group header row appears again at the top of page 2 above those 3 remaining rows.\n\nCollapsing a group hides rows, which changes the total page count:\n\n- While the table is in the loading state after a collapse, the **total number of pages also enters a skeleton loading state** because the page count needs to be recalculated.\n- After loading completes, the updated page count is displayed (for example, “of 20 pages” may become “of 19 pages” after collapsing a group).\n\nGrouping is **fundamentally incompatible with keyset pagination**. Keyset pagination filters rows based on the last seen value of the sort column, fetching only the next slice of individually ordered rows. Grouping requires the database to first collect all rows belonging to the same group, aggregate them, and return the collapsed result. These two concepts conflict because keyset pagination relies on a stable, unique cursor value per row, but grouped results are aggregated summaries that no longer correspond to individual rows with unique cursor values. A group may span multiple pages of the underlying data, so there is no reliable way to point a cursor at “the last group seen” using a row-level column value.\n\n\n\n## Selection with groups\n\nhttps://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=3486-52279&t=2PBanDq6QA2yNcI2-4\n\nThe checkbox in the group header row selects or deselects **all rows in the group**. All child row checkboxes within that group become checked or unchecked accordingly.\n\nIf only some rows within a group are selected, the group header checkbox displays a **“child selected” (indeterminate) state**, shown as a dash instead of a checkmark. This occurs both when the user deselects individual rows from a fully selected group and when the user selects individual rows without selecting the full group.\n\nGroups can span multiple pages. When the user selects a multi-page group via its header checkbox, **all items in the group are selected, including those not currently visible on the page**. The selection persists when navigating to subsequent pages. Rows belonging to the selected group on other pages also appear checked.\n\nBecause of this requirement, a table that has both selection and grouping **cannot use simple selection**. It must use **persistent selection**.\n\n### Backend dependency for group selection\n\nSelecting an entire group through the header checkbox likely requires backend support. When a group spans multiple pages, the frontend only has access to the rows currently loaded on the page. It cannot know the full set of items in the group without querying the backend. The backend needs to resolve which rows belong to the group and return them so the selection state is accurate across all pages.\n\nIf the backend does not support group-level selection, remove the checkbox from the group header row. Users can still select individual rows within a group, but they cannot select the entire group at once. This avoids a misleading interaction where the user expects all items in the group to be selected but only the currently loaded rows are affected.\n\nGrouping can also be used in tables that have no selection at all. In this case, no checkboxes appear on group headers or data rows. Only action icons (for example, edit or delete) appear on rows.\n\n";
    ds:tier ds:apps;
    ds:documentationStage ds:tag.needsdefinition;
    ds:hasProperty [
        a ds:Property;
        ds:name "grouping-columns";
        ds:summary "The set of columns that support grouping. Only columns with a small, known set of values are valid. The user selects which column to group by through the settings menu.";
        ds:propertyType "list of text";
        ds:constraints "Inherited from the data table. Each value must reference an enumerated-value column.";
        ds:optional "true"
    ], [
        a ds:Property;
        ds:name "default-group-column";
        ds:summary "The column the table is grouped by on initial load.";
        ds:propertyType "text";
        ds:constraints "Inherited from the data table. Must reference a column in grouping-columns.";
        ds:optional "true"
    ], [
        a ds:Property;
        ds:name "group-order";
        ds:summary "The order in which groups are displayed. Use a custom order when there is meaning associated with a specific sequence (for example, a status column ordered from start to finish).";
        ds:propertyType "choice";
        ds:constraints "Inherited from the data table. When custom, a custom order must be provided.";
        ds:optional "true";
        ds:defaultValue "alphabetical"
    ], [
        a ds:Property;
        ds:name "groups-expanded-by-default";
        ds:summary "Whether groups are expanded or collapsed on initial load.";
        ds:propertyType "boolean";
        ds:constraints "Inherited from the data table.";
        ds:optional "true";
        ds:defaultValue "true"
    ], [
        a ds:Property;
        ds:name "expanded";
        ds:summary "Whether this individual group is currently expanded or collapsed. The chevron points down when expanded and right when collapsed. Selecting the group header row toggles this state.";
        ds:propertyType "boolean";
        ds:optional "true";
        ds:defaultValue "Inherited from groups-expanded-by-default."
    ], [
        a ds:Property;
        ds:name "show-group-checkbox";
        ds:summary "Whether a selection checkbox is shown on the group header row. When the backend does not support group-level selection, set to false to avoid a misleading interaction. Users can still select individual rows within the group.";
        ds:propertyType "boolean";
        ds:constraints "Requires persistent selection when used with grouping. Requires backend support for group-level selection across pages.";
        ds:optional "true";
        ds:defaultValue "true when selection is enabled."
    ];
    ds:parentComponent ds:apps.pattern.datatable.
